.TH STREAM 5
.SH NAME
stream \- the parts that the programs write and read
.SH DESCRIPTION
A stream holds parts: the folded blank that
.IR smfold (1)
writes, the solids that a pipe of
.IR extrude (1),
.IR bool (1)
and
.IR fillet (1)
passes on, or the parts of an assembly in place, as
.IR place (1)
writes them.
Every program that reads parts reads a stream; its file name ends in
.BR .st .
A stream has one record on each line, in mm, and begins with the line
.BR PliCad :
.PP
.RS
.nf
PliCad
source PATH
material NAME R G B ROUGHNESS DENSITY [FINISH]
part NAME
  place R00 R01 R02 R10 R11 R12 R20 R21 R22 TX TY TZ
  base MATERIAL
  coat MATERIAL near|far edges|- N X0 Y0 ... XN-1 YN-1
  finish MATERIAL REF...
  thread REF [deep D] DESIGNATION...
  a folded blank, or an exact solid
  tol NAME number|length|angle LO HI
  vary NAME \-|+ RECORD...
end
tol NAME number|length|angle LO HI
vary NAME \-|+ face FACE SURFACE SURFACE
@N TEXT
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B source
names the source file of a pipe, whose lines that no program of the
pipe has taken yet follow as
.B @
lines, each with its number in the source; the last program of a pipe
refuses any that is left.
A
.B material
gives the color and the roughness of a material, from 0 to 1, its
density in g/cm3, and its finish, as
.IR plicad (7)
says.
A part has its place in the world (the identity in a pipe), the
material of its body, if any, and its coats: each a region of the flat
blank, a polygon of N corners, on the near side or the far one, and
with
.B edges
on the cut edges in it too, that takes the material; a later coat lies
over an earlier one.
A
.B finish
of a solid gives the faces that its references name
.RI ( part (5))
the material of a finish, the later over the earlier, and a
.B thread
the holes of the faces its reference names a thread
.RI ( part (5)),
its depth D in mm; the sets of faces of the source are written as their
references, a thread for each.
The material of the body must be one of the stream; that of a coat or
a finish need not be, and then it has no color.
A part with neither a blank nor a solid is an empty solid, as a
.B common
of two solids apart leaves.
.SS A folded blank
A blank is the flat part, cut into faces along the edges of its bend
zones, with a map for each face from flat (x, y, z), z in [\-t, 0], to
the part:
.PP
.RS
.nf
t T
map I rigid R00 ... R22 TX TY TZ
map I bend PARENT QX QY MX MY BA ANGLE R
face I MAP
vertex I X Y
edge I V0 V1 LEFT RIGHT [arc CX CY] [by FEATURE]
hole FACE CX CY R [csink RC H near|far] [blind D near|far] [by FEATURE]
insert NAME TYPE near|far HOLE D H [fills] [material M]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Indices start at 0 for each kind of record, and \- stands for no face.
A blind hole opens on the near face or the far one and goes D into the
sheet; it has no countersink.
An insert names the type of its hardware
.RI ( sm (5)),
the side its body stands on, its hole, and its body: a cylinder D
across and H high on that side over the hole, and with
.B fills
the hole too, through the sheet or to the floor of a blind hole, as a
flush or a blind type has it; H may be 0 then.
Its material is one of the stream.
The record holds all that a reader needs, which looks up no table.
A program that reads the parts of a stream gives each insert its body,
an exact solid, as a solid part PART/NAME after its part, at the place
of the part and of its material, if it has one; so measure, interfere,
tostep and tomesh take the bodies too.
A stream written from those parts holds the bodies, not the inserts.
A slab is mapped by a rotation and a translation; a bend zone, a
rectangle across the full width BA wide that begins on the line
through (QX, QY) perpendicular to the unit vector (MX, MY) into the
zone, is wrapped round the bend axis of its parent slab.
Arcs run counterclockwise from V0 to V1, and the face on the left of an
edge sees its boundary counterclockwise.
The feature of an edge or a hole is the name of the statement of the
source that made it, which the drawings select by
.RI ( draw (5)).
.SS An exact solid
A solid is given by its vertices, its edges on curves and its faces on
surfaces, in the frame of the part:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v X Y Z
e V0 V1 T0 T1 CURVE
f NAME SENSE SURFACE
l [+|\-]E ...
.fi
.RE
.PP
CURVE is
.BI line " O X" ,
.BI circle " O N X R" ,
.BI ellipse " O N X A B"
or
.BI nodal " O N X R RT" ,
and SURFACE
.BI plane " O N X" ,
.BI cylinder " O N X R" ,
.BI cone " O N X R TAN" ,
.BI sphere " O N X R" ,
.BI torus " O N X R RT"
or
.BI pipe " O N X A B RT" ,
O, N and X three numbers each.
A pipe is the surface of a ball of radius RT whose center runs along
the ellipse O N X A B; a point of it lies at RT from the point of the
ellipse nearest it, so that a face of a pipe lies where the tube does
not fold over itself.
For A = B it is the torus O N X A RT.
A nodal section is the curve where the torus O N X R RT meets the plane
at R \- RT along X, which touches the inner circle of the torus: a
figure of eight with its node at T = 0, the point of touch,
.PP
.RS
.nf
O + (R \- RT) X + u(T) Y + RT sin(T) N
u(T) = sin(T/2) sqrt(2 RT (2 R \- RT \- RT cos(T)))
.fi
.RE
.PP
for T from \-2 pi to 2 pi, Y = N \(mu X; a round ends on such a curve
where its face runs on smoothly into the face it ends on
.RI ( fillet (1)).
Each face is followed by its loops, counterclockwise about its outward
normal, the natural normal of its surface times SENSE.
The faces are named as
.IR part (5)
says.
.SS Ranges
The tolerances of the source
.RI ( plicad (7))
give a part its ranges.
A record
.B tol
names a parameter that the part leans on, what it measures, and the
ends of its range less its value, LO not above HI, in mm or degrees.
Each record
.B vary
after it gives a number of the part at the low end of the range of
the parameter,
.BR \- ,
or at the high one,
.BR + ,
where that differs from the part, as the tool that made the part
found it: it built the part again with the parameter moved by
1/10000 of its range, the others as they are, and each number moves
in proportion to its end.
That is exact where the part is linear in the parameter, as a blank
is in each length: its edges, holes and bend lines lie at sums of
lengths and of bend allowances, linear in the thickness and the
radius; for an angle it is right to first order.
A record of a blank is one of
.PP
.RS
.nf
t T
map I rigid R00 ... R22 TX TY TZ
map I bend PARENT QX QY MX MY BA ANGLE R
vertex I X Y
edge I CX CY
hole I CX CY R RC H D
coat I X0 Y0 ... XN\-1 YN\-1
.fi
.RE
.PP
the thickness, a map, a vertex, the center of an arc, a hole (with RC
R and H 0 without a countersink, D 0 through), or the corners of a coat
at that end, by the index of their record.
A part rebuilt so is of the same form: a rebuild of another form, or
one that moves a number more than 10000 times the parameter, is
refused.
A record of a solid,
.B face
FACE SURFACE SURFACE, moves each face of the name FACE on the first
surface to the second; as the faces keep their names and surfaces down
a pipe of
.IR bool (1)
and
.IR fillet (1),
these records stand outside the parts in it, for every part of the
pipe, and
.IR place (1)
puts them in each solid part.
The faces that
.I fillet
makes have none.
.SH SEE ALSO
plicad(7), smfold(1), place(1), part(5), assembly(5)
